         ATTRIBUTION IS REQUIRED BY THIS LICENCE and is given by this
         paragraph; keep it with the file.
19 joints, 370 vertices, 256 triangles, one 1.25 s clip. Small enough to be
a test's reference numbers, and authored by nobody here — which is the whole
point of a fixture for a READER.

WHAT THESE FILES ARE FOR, AND WHAT THEY ARE NOT
------------------------------------------------
They are TEMPORARY REFERENCES for the import-and-skinning pipeline: real
skeletons, real weights and real clips, so the importer, the .dfo v5 sections,
the retarget onto our rig and the skinned vertex program can be proved end to
end against files nobody in this project authored.

They are NOT the game's character. Daggerfall N's own character is later work;
when it lands, these keep one job — the pipeline's regression fixtures.

THE GAME NEVER READS THE .gltf OR THE .glb. The importer bakes them into .dfo
and the runtime reads only that (в1, the tooling pivot: nothing is generated —
or parsed — in the frame). Re-bake with:

    dfn_import_gltf assets/objects/characters/Knight.glb \
        --out assets/objects/characters/Knight.dfo \
        --name knight-reference --height 2.0

    dfn_import_gltf assets/objects/characters/RiggedFigure.gltf \
        --out assets/objects/characters/RiggedFigure.dfo \
        --name reference-figure --height 1.8 --yaw 0

WHY THE KNIGHT IS FITTED TO 2.00 m AND NOT TO PLAYER_CAPSULE_HEIGHT (1.8).
He is a stylised chibi: his head is a fifth of him and his plume another
0.5 m. Fitting his BOUNDING BOX to 1.8 would give a man whose body reads at
1.4 m, and fitting his HIP JOINT to ours (--fit-hips, the right tool for a
proportional rig) asks for a 5.4 m giant — the importer says so out loud. At
2.00 m of bounding box his body stands at about 1.8 and the plume rises above
it, which is what a knight with a plume looks like.
